WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.140
- Hey good humans.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.360
I'm Dan Chen, director
of the Jubilee produced

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.280
feature documentary, "accepted".

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.870
A story that follows four students

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.720
at the controversial T.M
Landry College Prep School,

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.330
and their high stakes quest
for elite college admission.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.550
We spent the last four years pitching,

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.890
filming, editing, and finishing this film.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.690
And we can't wait to share
this extraordinary story

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.090
with you as told by the
students who lived through it.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.030
Film comes out on July
1st in select theaters,

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.360
and you can also find it
available on Amazon and AppleTV.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.580
Now enjoy the episode.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.590
- [Announcer] Middle Ground
is a social experiment

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.020
that brings humans with
opposing beliefs together.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.910
These discussions may contain viewpoints

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.830
that are the result of misinformation.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.170
Remember to seek out
experts and to be critical

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.420
of your own biases while
forming an opinion.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.910
Please see the humanity
in each participant,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.610
and as always, we encourage empathy.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.380
- My grandmother being
a college professor,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.620
when she and my grandfather
graduated from college

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.030
and earned their master's degrees,

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.600
they were literally able
to take that to the bank

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.760
as a down payment on a home.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.471
A college degree meant guaranteed income.

00:01:03.471 --> 00:01:06.054
(upbeat music)

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.913
- [Interviewer] Step forward
if you agree with the prompt,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.216
my parents expected me to go to college.

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:25.883
(feet clomping)

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.710
- Even though my dad didn't
go to college himself,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.550
he went through a little bit
of an associate's degree,

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.530
but his main thing was a photographer.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.220
And then my mom being from
Jamaica, completely different,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.360
education being her way of getting out of

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.000
that kind of environment,
poverty, things like that.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
She always wanted me to go to school

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.160
and they both put me
through private school

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.780
to better prepare me for college,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.440
so they kind of started me off on the path

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.670
as soon as they could.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.550
- Growing up I actually
didn't even realize

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:10.530
there was an option to not attend college.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.570
I thought that part was mandatory,

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.280
but you may or may not graduate.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.530
It was very important to
my grandmother as well,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.940
being a college professor,

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.870
and when she and my grandfather
graduated from college

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.400
and earned their master's degrees,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.000
they were literally able
to take that to the bank

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.160
as a down payment on a home.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.990
At the time a college degree
meant guaranteed income.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.930
- My dad was always the
type of person to say,

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.000
get your degree, I
don't care what it's in.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.100
So I always felt a little
bit of flexibility about

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.930
what specifically I wanted to do,

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.390
and that's why I kind of
took community college

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.940
as a time to try a lot of different things

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.210
and see what I'm interested
in and not break the bank.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.100
My name is Pam and I'm pro college.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.710
The main thing that I
feel like I missed out on

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.634
by going to community college first

00:02:57.634 --> 00:03:00.090
was that I wasn't given the opportunity

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.510
to pursue leadership roles in like clubs,

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.280
or things that I was a part of.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.160
There is a bit of a stigma
with transfer students

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.670
that you weren't there
on campus long enough.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.760
I encountered a lot of
people who would discuss

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.170
feeling imposter syndrome

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.090
or feeling like they weren't good enough

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.500
to be at a school like UCLA,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.300
that they couldn't do
as well as the people

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.890
who had been there all four years.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.190
And I definitely felt that
feeling sometimes as well.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.740
- [Interviewer] Can the
disagreers or step forward.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.250
- There was no expectation for me

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.480
to go to college whatsoever.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.820
So it was really my
personal decision to go.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.370
I saw a lot of poverty in my
community, in my neighborhood,

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.160
and so how do I avoid this
situation as an adult?

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.300
There was no plan B for me,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.980
so it was like, if I don't do it,

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.020
I'm going back to my hometown

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.460
and I'll be working at
a factory somewhere,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.283
or I can do it and see what happens.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.450
- [Interviewer] College's
use of affirmative action

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.133
is problematic.

00:04:00.398 --> 00:04:02.100
(feet clomping)

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:02.933
- Okay.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:08.040
So my personal experience
with affirmative action

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.820
when I went to the PWI,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.300
I was chosen pretty much
based on affirmative action,

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.160
there was a lot of heavy marketing,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.320
hey, come to our school,
come to our school.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.340
Affirmative action can be problematic

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.310
in the sense that when
these students reach campus

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.500
there is no place for them.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.540
- Yeah, it's like, okay, they have clubs,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.180
they have other people that
they could reach out to,

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.890
but it's more like once we get you in,

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:35.723
you're on your own,
- You're on your own-

00:04:35.723 --> 00:04:36.750
- You gotta figure it out by yourself.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.610
And I also think it's being problematic

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.660
when it comes to society,

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.340
'cause now you're having a lot of

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.080
white Americans saying that,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.150
the only reason this
person got into this school

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.320
was because of this reason.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.170
- Especially private institutions,
these Ivy league schools,

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.150
certain types of people
attend these schools.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.630
If you don't necessarily
fit their standards,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.110
then once again, you can be
ostracized from that group.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.510
My name is Zakiya and I'm pro college.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:04.960
Not going to college is
a privilege depending on

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.400
who you are and who you know.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.910
I feel like colleges support minorities

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.220
depending on where you are.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.590
For me personally, I'm from Baltimore,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:20.280
there were opportunities for minorities,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.070
of course it's a majority minority city.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:26.130
If I was in another state

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:29.880
that was maybe more conservative
or predominantly white,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.934
I do feel like I would not
have had as many opportunities.

00:05:32.934 --> 00:05:35.601
(feet clomping)

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.330
- Meritocracy can't
exist in the same place

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.530
where you have nepotism and
legacies and all of that.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.790
But there's always some
sort of an action involved

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:52.620
in getting people into universities.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.017
I personally know someone
who attended classes

00:05:56.017 --> 00:05:58.830
at a certain Ivy league university

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:03.240
with a future president and
he was a terrible student,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.210
but of course he got in
because he was legacy

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.613
and his family donated a building.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.610
And that's how you become
president, that's America.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.870
Affirmative action is good
to give people an opportunity

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.240
who wouldn't otherwise have it.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.440
- I think the intention is good

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.410
and you can't speak to intention

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:23.940
when the system looks the way it does.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.950
- We also have to factor in the aspect

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.290
of finances and money
and schools making money.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:32.100
And when we're talking about intention,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.380
yes, there might be one
professor that's like,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.850
oh, I'm so glad you're here,

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.610
but that doesn't speak
for the entire university,

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.020
that doesn't speak for the board.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:43.530
Is the intention to give us
minorities an opportunity

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:46.470
or is the intention to
not hear complaints?

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.510
- Mm-hmm, to boost their numbers,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.040
boost their diversity levels.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.300
- Well, I actually think
about it differently,

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.160
I think affirmitive action,

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.140
it definitely works against nepotism.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.080
And I feel like if it were
not on affirmative action,

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.690
a lot of black students
wouldn't be able to go to PWIs.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:02.910
And so I agree that, once you get there,

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.950
there is a difficulty
being a student of color

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.690
on a predominantly white institution.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.000
With that position it
also was the other side

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.250
that would say that same
thing, they would say,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.920
we won't let you in because
you won't be a good fit here,

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.660
because you won't fit the culture,

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:17.730
and that's the way that
they would use to bar people

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:18.600
from being able to go.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.050
And so, I won't say, it's
better for them to turn us away

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:23.700
because they don't have the resources.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.130
Let me get there and let me
figure that out for myself,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.620
versus you telling me that
I won't be a good fit there.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:30.180
And that's what affirmative action allows.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.320
And I also will say,

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.150
white women benefit from
an affirmative action

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.770
more than any other minority group.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.990
It's not necessarily
about students of color

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.610
specifically being allowed on campus,

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.893
it's about minorities being allowed.

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:46.219
- [Interviewer] A degree doesn't
qualify you for most jobs.

00:07:46.219 --> 00:07:48.886
(feet clomping)

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:54.450
- I think where we're seeing
the world currently go

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:55.860
in terms of the job market is,

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.470
a lot of employers are
hiring for experience,

00:07:58.470 --> 00:07:59.910
they're hiring to see,

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.500
do you know what you're talking about?

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.590
And that's where I think
internships really come into play,

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.810
and that's why a lot of college students

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.490
are going for internships,

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.890
is they need to go out there
and get that experience

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.740
so that way employers want to hire them.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.660
No employer is going to turn you down

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.940
if you are like, Hey, I
have all of this experience,

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.730
or Hey, I know how to do
exactly what you're hiring for,

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:23.340
but oh, I didn't go to
college for four years.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.530
- With having that
listed as a qualification

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:26.820
and a requirement,

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.610
kind of is like a gate
keeping thing more so

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.750
than who's going to perform
the best on the job.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.330
I'm Michelle and I'm anti college.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.130
I became anti college

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:41.910
when I graduated high school valedictorian

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:46.320
and I realized that
continuing on with college

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.390
would be a debt sentence.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.117
My godmother actually
gave me a book called,

00:08:52.117 --> 00:08:53.820
"The Education of Millionaires"

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.610
and she hadn't read the book in advance,

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.580
so she kind of thought it
was going to encourage me

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.530
to invest in college.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:05.520
But what the book actually
did was explain the stories

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.190
of a lot of people who became millionaires

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.031
without formal education.

00:09:11.031 --> 00:09:13.698
(feet clomping)

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.850
- Most companies don't take
the time to read that resume,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.050
they feed it through a machine.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:25.170
If you talk the talk
and know certain people

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:26.280
to Jimmy your way in there,

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.250
you don't really need the
qualifications to do the job.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.950
People who have the
qualifications don't get the job.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:33.960
- I've been job hunting
over the last couple months.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:35.250
I started like a spreadsheet

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.430
where I was putting how
many of them are asking for

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.530
X amount of years of experience?

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.810
Almost all of them were
college degree needed.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.240
I still even got maybe three or four

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:47.700
for every 40 that I applied to.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:48.840
- I'm originally from Miami

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.010
and I used to work for the County,

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:52.590
and I used to work for social events.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:53.910
And now that I've moved here,

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.500
it's really hard for me to transition

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:57.540
towards the same field
because I tell people,

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.090
Hey, look, I used to run
football tournaments,

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:01.620
I used to host food drives,

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:05.010
but because I don't have
a degree in marketing

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.510
or in event coordination,

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:09.360
people, like you said, when
I put on Indeed, they say,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.250
Hey, you need a degree to get in.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:12.600
But because I don't have that degree,

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.100
I'm not even allowed in the door.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:16.080
My name is Kenny and I'm pro college.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:17.970
Going to college is really important to me

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.400
because I've had a long
line of family members

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:21.930
who have been educators.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.020
My mom who passed away
from cancer was a teacher,

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.400
and when she came to this country,

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.670
the only dream that she had
for me was to go to college.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.380
In the early stages of me going to school

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:34.530
it felt like a very hard burden to carry,

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.450
the fact that I don't
know what I want to do

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.340
when I go to school but
I just have to do it

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.050
because it's somebody else's wishes.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:43.740
But eventually when I started
growing up and realizing that

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.750
I have my own reasons
for going to college,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.490
and on top of that as well,

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:49.800
can fulfill my mom's dream

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.330
was something that
really kind of pushed me

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.520
towards wanting to continue my education

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.350
and going to school.

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:59.413
- [Interviewer] All student
loan debt should be forgiven.

00:10:59.413 --> 00:11:02.080
(feet clomping)

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:07.140
- It's crazy to me that
you go for four years

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:08.640
and you are paying off

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:10.290
hundreds of thousands of dollars in debt,

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:14.820
and you might actually
meet all of the payments

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:17.490
and you still have like double to pay.

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:19.320
A really popular payment plan

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:20.640
that we've been seeing,

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.680
Purdue university started doing this,

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:23.790
it's called income share agreements.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:28.790
So instead of paying for
your degree up front,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:32.190
if college actually
helps you land that job,

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:34.410
you start to pay a small
percentage of your salary

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.240
over the span of a few years.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.000
And after five, six, seven years,

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:40.500
whatever percentage you pay, great,

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.660
if not, and you're not
making a lot of money,

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.400
then like the college actually failed you

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.170
in helping you get that career

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:47.640
that it was supposed
to in the first place.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.050
- I like that, it puts accountability

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.720
on the institution where it should be.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.170
- Absolutely.
- Absolutely.

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:53.940
- Not the borrower.
- It's very easy to be like,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.880
well, in like the Nordic
countries, you pay $5

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.350
and you get your degree.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:00.030
It's easy, but it's different here,

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:01.470
and it has been, and it will be,

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:03.600
unless some big change happens,

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.130
but I don't know what that is,

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.500
I don't know how do we
address the millennials

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:08.333
who went through college

00:12:08.333 --> 00:12:09.870
and have racked up billions in debt,

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.040
and how do we change it

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:13.290
to make it better for our
future generations too.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:14.670
- And I really think it should be canceled

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:15.870
just because of the pretenses

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:17.820
in which the loans were
actually given out.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.800
It's literally the definition
of predatory lending

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.500
in terms of giving thousands of dollars

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.483
to someone with no credit score-

00:12:24.483 --> 00:12:25.316
- Yeah, 18 year olds.

00:12:25.316 --> 00:12:27.930
- Yeah. And so, when they get those loans

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.280
and they don't really understand,

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:31.230
the interest is racking up

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:33.270
and then six months after you graduate

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:34.470
and whether you have a job or not,

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.019
you're gonna have to
start paying 'em back.

00:12:36.019 --> 00:12:39.240
(feet clomping)

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:40.950
- Why y'all? What's going on?

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:45.950
- My disagreement is in
the way that it's handled.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:51.120
There is this particular
scam which is widespread.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.190
People have gotten
caught up in other scams

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:57.210
and those are not just
immediately forgiven.

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:58.860
Now I do understand
that it's kind of like,

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.312
it's a different situation
because of how much pressure

00:13:01.312 --> 00:13:05.010
was placed upon you
getting into this scam,

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:07.710
even governmental pressure as well,

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:11.293
which is why they bear
some responsibility in it.

00:13:11.293 --> 00:13:13.170
- I was a victim of one of these scams.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:14.250
I took a few loans out,

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:16.590
and then my last year
I didn't have enough,

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:20.340
and so I took a 5k loan and
that one was the worst one.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:23.100
What helped me back from stepping
forward was the word, all.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.140
Some people might have
taken out that loan,

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:25.973
gone to college,

00:13:25.973 --> 00:13:28.590
and now they're making tons of money

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.810
that they can afford to pay it back.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.270
If somebody's capable of contributing,

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:35.430
then why wouldn't they?

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:38.040
I'm Daniel, and I'm anti college.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.120
I took a lot of different classes

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:40.200
while I was in art school,

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:44.700
and I gained, I guess,
a wider set of skills

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.020
than my other brother did.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.060
The thing that felt unfair to me was that

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.370
it cost the same for us
to go through college,

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:53.970
yet, I got a better return
on my schooling than he did.

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:56.070
And he still has to pay the same amount

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:57.000
that I was having to pay.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.920
If someone's unsure of their career path,

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:02.130
is it worth them going hundreds
of thousand dollars in debt

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:04.740
when they don't know what
they're gonna do with their life?

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:07.572
- [Interviewer] College
campuses are radicalizing

00:14:07.572 --> 00:14:09.191
young people.

00:14:09.191 --> 00:14:11.858
(feet clomping)

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.060
- I hesitated at first

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.680
because I was interpreting that as

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:19.470
the universities influencing people,

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:20.790
but I don't think that's the case.

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:23.310
I think people who might
have certain beliefs

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:25.800
are going to college and just
spreading it to their peers.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:28.590
So in that regard, I
would say college campuses

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:29.880
are contributing to that.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:30.810
- When you see a headline

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:33.720
and you see that a
college is pursuing this,

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.040
or it's agreeing with this,

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:38.280
I feel like it has a lot
more damage towards a story

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.400
as if the school is promoting
this type of behavior.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.770
And I feel like because they
notice that a lot of people

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.950
are getting a lot of tension
for those specific schools,

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.307
it's like, okay, let's give
a more incentive to do it

00:14:49.307 --> 00:14:51.390
'cause it's helping
our name get out there.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.550
- If you're not in these spaces

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.780
where you're hearing a lot
of arguments from every side,

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.990
it's easy to be like, well,
that doesn't apply to me,

00:14:57.990 --> 00:14:58.980
so why would I care?

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:01.500
I think for me, at least being
in a college environment,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.750
I was forced to see these things

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:06.150
and more exposed than I
would've been if I hadn't gone.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.500
- When I heard the word radical,

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:08.970
I like paused it for a second

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:10.890
'cause I think it has like
a negative connotation

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:11.880
just in the media,

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:13.500
and so I feel like when
you hear people say,

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:14.333
radical anything,

00:15:14.333 --> 00:15:16.920
you're like, okay, that
sounds like something bad.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.270
I feel like it's really the education,

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:20.310
they're learning more about the society

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:23.250
and about the history of
the government in America.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:25.020
And so that does sometimes make you angry

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:26.190
and makes you want to speak out

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:30.093
and become an activist for
a certain topic or position.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:34.590
- In my mind though, it's no different

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:36.900
than what algorithms are perpetuating

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:38.370
on social media right now.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:39.810
If you're on Twitter,

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.370
there you're going to be following people

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:42.690
that are within your space

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.730
and you're gonna be seeing that content.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:47.220
And I think that's actually
playing more of a role

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:52.020
in radicalizing and doing
more in the political space

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:54.510
than what we're actually
seeing at college campuses.

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.520
- Me personally, I grew
up in a conservative

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:57.750
Christian household.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.130
Growing up in those environments,

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:03.120
I feel like radicalization,
it really starts at home.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.520
It starts with what your
parents tell you about people.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.043
I think that when we get to college,

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:12.690
we're in more of a free
space to voice these opinions

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:14.433
and even on social media,

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:17.070
there's also a level of anonymity,

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:20.550
so people feel more comfortable
sharing certain viewpoints.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:22.050
I feel like these things start at home

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:23.520
and they're just pushed
more to the forefront

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:25.680
once you get a little older.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.070
- [Interviewer] Colleges
prioritize making money

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:33.242
over providing quality education.

00:16:33.242 --> 00:16:35.704
(feet clomping)

00:16:35.704 --> 00:16:38.487
- Oh, we all agree how nice.

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:42.960
- As someone who works at a
college, it's a business first,

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:44.340
and I see that first hand.

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.590
I think it really does come
down to the bottom line

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:48.900
for a lot of institutions in
terms of them making money.

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:50.340
And so they will make certain decisions

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:52.350
in terms of, what will
allow us to make more money,

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.750
what makes us look better
as an institute versus,

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:56.130
necessarily the care of the student

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.320
being the primary concern.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.862
- The teachers that I
had at community college

00:17:00.862 --> 00:17:03.420
were no better or worse
than the ones at UCLA,

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:04.290
sometimes better.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:06.240
They were nicer, they
were more approachable,

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:07.200
smaller class size,

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.360
more classes available
in community college.

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:12.930
When I got to UCLA, I thought
like there's one class,

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:14.100
and it's only at 5:00 PM.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:15.390
Are you kidding me?

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:17.040
How am I supposed to make
a schedule about that?

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.650
So I kind of realized a lot
of things when I got to UCLA

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:22.260
that like, this is not exactly
what I thought it would be.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:24.270
- The colleges that I graduated from,

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:26.610
they had a heavy focus on freshmen.

00:17:26.610 --> 00:17:28.620
It was all about freshman,
freshman, freshman freshmen.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:31.470
Bring in more freshmen
because the graduation rate

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:32.370
is already so low.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:33.720
Freshmen bring in money,

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:35.190
freshmen are there the whole year.

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:36.600
And then if they stay, they stay,

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:37.560
if they don't, they don't.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:39.720
They were changing all
of the dorm buildings

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:42.690
to freshman dorms, so that
only freshmen have housing.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:44.340
- Can you talk about the
quality of dorms too?

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:45.508
- Oh.

00:17:45.508 --> 00:17:46.341
(all laughing)

00:17:46.341 --> 00:17:47.174
Trash, trash.
- For this,

00:17:47.174 --> 00:17:48.007
tens of thousand dollars you're paying-

00:17:48.007 --> 00:17:49.020
- Terrible.
- Yes.

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:50.130
- How does that make sense?

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:51.810
- Also when you look at public institution

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:53.730
and you see the salaries,

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.380
90% of the time you're gonna see that

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:58.200
the highest paid person
is a college coach.

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:00.750
And that'll show you where
the actual money is going to,

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:02.220
and it's like millions of dollars

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:04.800
more than every other person
that works at the institute.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.960
My name is KC I am pro college.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.930
College was a opportunity
for me, definitely,

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:12.720
with the ability to let me outta poverty.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:16.410
I grew up in a single parent
household, raised by my mom,

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.840
and so I think that just
the ability to go to college

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.640
and earn a degree,

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:23.400
set me up for higher paying jobs

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.740
and the ability to pay my mom's bill,

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.143
as well as be a better
person in my community.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:33.063
- [Interviewer] The college
acceptance system is rigged.

00:18:33.925 --> 00:18:36.592
(feet clomping)

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.160
- So I went to one of these events

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.740
that the school bell
was always talking about

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:45.510
just to learn more about
the admission process.

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:47.760
And my mom ran into one
of these acquaintances

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:49.290
she met from like back in the day,

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:53.220
and he had put all of his
children through the school.

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:54.540
And one of the things he told us

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:57.180
was that the school made
it super competitive

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:01.020
to get in so that their
acceptance rate looks like, 8%.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:04.800
But for transfers, it's much higher,

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:06.150
like that's the way into,

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:07.710
I would argue most of these schools,

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:09.900
if you don't have the proper grades

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.000
or maybe you can't afford it that year,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.140
there's back doors,

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:15.120
and just hearing that I'm assuming

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:16.530
there are more back doors.

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:18.150
- My dream college when
I was in high school

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:19.920
is I really wanted to go to Georgia Tech.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:24.000
I started my college admissions
process in West Virginia

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:27.270
and going and visiting
the college counselor

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:29.250
on Georgia Tech's campus, they were like,

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:30.090
this is what you need to do,

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:30.923
these are what you need to hit,

00:19:30.923 --> 00:19:32.460
this is how many APS you need to have.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:35.457
My junior year, my parents
moved us to Tennessee

00:19:35.457 --> 00:19:40.290
and I ended up applying to
Georgia Tech from Tennessee

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:43.800
and I got denied and I was heartbroken

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.080
because I had met all of the stats

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.730
that she had told me to meet.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:51.120
I ended up calling my admissions
counselor at Georgia Tech

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:53.040
because I'd developed a
relationship with her.

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:54.960
And I remember crying with her in the car

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.060
and just being like what happened?

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.680
And she straight up told me that

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:01.410
if you would've applied
from West Virginia,

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.080
from your school, you would've gotten in.

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:06.663
My name's Mehak, and I'm anti college.

00:20:06.663 --> 00:20:08.730
When I first entered college,

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:11.610
I thought that you had
to have a college degree

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:13.860
to be successful and to
start your own business.

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:15.840
And I met a bunch of people

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:17.100
that had dropped out of college

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:21.060
that were between the ages of 18 and 23,

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:22.470
that was eyeopening to me

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:24.240
because there were people that had raised

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:26.220
millions of dollars to
start their own companies,

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.350
and they didn't have
that college background.

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:31.500
My mom has her master's,
my dad is a doctor,

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:33.480
so for me to turn around and say,

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:36.030
Hey, I actually don't even think I need

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:37.200
four years of college,

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.660
I think I can actually just go
out and do things on my own.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:42.905
It was a big cultural shock for them.

00:20:42.905 --> 00:20:45.572
(feet clomping)

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.093
- I think about college as like,

00:20:49.109 --> 00:20:49.942
what we said in terms of being a business.

00:20:49.942 --> 00:20:51.300
And I feel like all businesses are rigged

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.310
to make as much money
as they possibly can.

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:54.630
The Institute makes more money

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:55.620
when students are outta state

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:56.820
and paying outta state tuition.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:58.740
Knowing what we know
about college acceptance,

00:20:58.740 --> 00:20:59.972
what would you all want to see?

00:20:59.972 --> 00:21:01.860
There's no way that every person

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.380
that wants to go to a
particular college can go.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:06.473
So it's like they have to find
some way to weed people out.

00:21:06.473 --> 00:21:09.180
- I think there needs to be
more transparency overall

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.590
with universities.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:12.150
Students really need to be aware

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:14.250
of what they're going to college to do,

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:17.065
who they are looking to learn from,

00:21:17.065 --> 00:21:18.360
I think will be beneficial.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.190
Can they learn from them
once they get there?

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.530
I'm Kyle and I'm anti college.

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:24.150
If I could do it all over again,

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:26.700
I would choose not to go to college

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:29.970
because there have been several benchmarks

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:33.840
that I can think of where
I've progressed creatively

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:36.360
that have happened
outside of the classroom,

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.240
in terms of experience
or things I have learned

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:41.760
from people working in the industry

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:45.690
or lessons that I have
discovered about myself,

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:49.560
just from sitting, doing
the craft that I want to do,

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.900
that more or less was
not taught in the classes

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:54.153
that I thought I would be taught.

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:57.990
- [Interviewer] We
should stop telling kids

00:21:57.990 --> 00:22:01.329
that they should all go to college.

00:22:01.329 --> 00:22:03.996
(feet clomping)

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:07.320
- Yeah, I think some kids,

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.620
if they are really, really
excelling in the school

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.600
then yeah, you want to encourage that one.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:14.160
But you'll always have that kid

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:15.720
that just has no idea what they want to do

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:17.820
and that person shouldn't
be pressured to go,

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:18.900
shouldn't even be encouraged to go,

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:21.360
they need to just figure out something.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:22.770
- Or if their skills are not conducive

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:24.960
to like a normal degree,
- Exactly.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:25.890
- Or go to trade school,

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:28.740
learn a trade and you'll
you'll make money.

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:31.020
- I think that we need to
switch away from saying,

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:33.390
telling every single kid that
you need to go to school,

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.090
more like, Hey, look, if you
don't want to go to school,

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:37.710
here are some other options.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:39.870
- Yeah, definitely like
more career focused,

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:41.030
more profession focused,

00:22:41.030 --> 00:22:42.660
especially in like some of our cases

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:44.280
when we're expected to go to college

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:46.290
and people don't care
what degree you have.

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:47.520
That's a little bit of a red flag

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:48.990
'cause you really need
to be intentional about

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:49.920
the choices you make.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:53.670
- College is no longer an
indicator of future success.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:54.630
With the internet,

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:57.540
with just all of this access to knowledge,

00:22:57.540 --> 00:23:00.810
you can create your own
future outside of college.

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:02.430
And I think it's really good

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:06.360
for helping you move into career paths

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:07.620
where like doctor, lawyer,

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:09.144
where like you do need a higher education.

00:23:09.144 --> 00:23:11.580
I think other than that,

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:14.190
I think it's a lot cheaper
and it makes a lot more sense

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:16.200
for people to find a trade school

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:19.350
or to go to their local community college

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:21.360
and just get the skills that they need to,

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.130
so that way they can start working.

00:23:23.130 --> 00:23:24.450
- I was gonna say, I
definitely don't think

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:26.250
that college is for everyone,

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:27.630
but I think that most of the time

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:29.790
when we hear conversations
of people saying,

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:30.630
you should go to college.

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:32.640
It's usually from people
who did not go to college,

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.301
it's like generic advice in terms of,

00:23:34.301 --> 00:23:35.820
you should do something with your life.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:37.050
That's really what
they're trying to tell you

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:37.980
and they just use college

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:40.560
because I do think it is
still an indicator of success.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:41.550
I know you said it's not,

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:43.860
statistically you are more likely

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:45.090
to make a million dollars more

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:46.680
than an average high school graduate. .

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:48.260
- [Interviewer] That's a
wrap on the group discussion.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:50.843
